Built-in color themes besides Runebook Dark and Runebook Light.

Obsidian Dark and Obsidian Light use Obsidian's published default palette.
The files are original. They are not copied from the Obsidian app.

Obsidian Dark
  File: themes/obsidian-dark.json
  Source: https://publish.obsidian.md/hub/04+-+Guides%2C+Workflows%2C+%26+Courses/Guides/Default+Obsidian+Theme+Colors

Obsidian Light
  File: themes/obsidian-light.json
  Source: https://publish.obsidian.md/hub/04+-+Guides%2C+Workflows%2C+%26+Courses/Guides/Default+Obsidian+Theme+Colors

Syntax colors for both files come from the Obsidian color palette.
Source: https://docs.obsidian.md/Reference/CSS+variables/Foundations/Colors

Blue Topaz Dark and Blue Topaz Light use the default palette from Blue Topaz.
The files keep `name`, `type`, `colors`, and `tokenColors`.

Blue Topaz Dark
  File: themes/blue-topaz-dark.json
  Source: https://github.com/PKM-er/Blue-Topaz_Obsidian-css
  License: MIT
  Copyright (c) 2020 whyt-byte

Blue Topaz Light
  File: themes/blue-topaz-light.json
  Source: https://github.com/PKM-er/Blue-Topaz_Obsidian-css
  License: MIT
  Copyright (c) 2020 whyt-byte

Each remaining file is one theme from a VS Code extension.
The file has no include. The app reads name, type, colors, and tokenColors.

These packs are not included:
- Monokai Pro. The license allows evaluation only. It forbids redistribution.
- Material Theme. The license forbids redistribution and derivative works.
The next open color theme is Theme (`tal7aouy.theme`).

